WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.440 --> 00:00:01.890
- Just a dimwit who lives off his mother,

00:00:01.890 --> 00:00:03.050
but you're with me in this place.

00:00:03.050 --> 00:00:04.470
Isn't that interesting?

00:00:04.470 --> 00:00:06.370
- [Narrator] This is the
voice that many people heard

00:00:06.370 --> 00:00:09.270
when watching Netflix's
most popular series ever,

00:00:09.270 --> 00:00:11.110
the South Korean drama, "Squid Game."

00:00:11.110 --> 00:00:12.450
- A man just died!

00:00:13.370 --> 00:00:14.203
- [Narrator] And English wasn't

00:00:14.203 --> 00:00:16.398
the only dubbed language available.

00:00:16.398 --> 00:00:17.764
(Gi-hun speaks Thai)

00:00:17.764 --> 00:00:19.559
(Gi-hun's friend speaks French)

00:00:19.559 --> 00:00:20.690
(machine beeps)

00:00:20.690 --> 00:00:21.718
There were 12 others.

00:00:21.718 --> 00:00:24.880
(machine speaks Portuguese)
(they both shout)

00:00:24.880 --> 00:00:27.500
- Dubbing is one of the hardest forms

00:00:27.500 --> 00:00:29.590
of voice work that there is.

00:00:29.590 --> 00:00:30.910
- [Narrator] This voice work has been key

00:00:30.910 --> 00:00:34.060
to making the South Korean
show a global phenomenon.

00:00:34.060 --> 00:00:37.280
The series was also
subtitled in 31 languages

00:00:37.280 --> 00:00:40.360
but Netflix says more people
watch "Squid Game" dubbed

00:00:40.360 --> 00:00:42.630
and overall, dubbing
consumption on the platform

00:00:42.630 --> 00:00:44.640
has more than doubled from last year,

00:00:44.640 --> 00:00:47.140
with help from some big hits.

00:00:47.140 --> 00:00:49.057
- In the past, people would say,

00:00:49.057 --> 00:00:51.480
"Oh, you can't really dub into English.

00:00:51.480 --> 00:00:54.260
The American audience
or the English audience,

00:00:54.260 --> 00:00:56.040
they don't like to hear dubbing."

00:00:56.040 --> 00:00:57.297
Well, look at us now.

00:00:57.297 --> 00:01:00.140
"Squid Game" is an amazing example.

00:01:00.140 --> 00:01:01.630
- [Narrator] But the dubbed
version of "Squid Game"

00:01:01.630 --> 00:01:03.633
has also drawn criticism online,

00:01:04.690 --> 00:01:07.620
so we got access to this
studio in Los Angeles

00:01:07.620 --> 00:01:09.720
to understand why getting dubbing right

00:01:09.720 --> 00:01:12.817
has become crucial to
Netflix's global expansion.

00:01:14.225 --> 00:01:15.392
- [Greg] Okay.

00:01:16.290 --> 00:01:18.890
- [Narrator] Greg Chun is the
English voice of Seong Gi-hun,

00:01:18.890 --> 00:01:20.550
a chauffeur with a gambling problem

00:01:20.550 --> 00:01:22.360
who, along with other indebted adults,

00:01:22.360 --> 00:01:24.670
is invited to play a
series of children's games

00:01:24.670 --> 00:01:27.488
for a chance to win a large cash prize.

00:01:27.488 --> 00:01:29.100
(Greg grunts)

00:01:29.100 --> 00:01:31.290
Chun has lent his voice to
characters in video games

00:01:31.290 --> 00:01:33.640
like "Judgment" and "Call of Duty".

00:01:33.640 --> 00:01:35.430
- [Soldier] Stitch, the rats are here.

00:01:35.430 --> 00:01:36.940
- [Narrator] But he says
dubbing for "Squid Game"

00:01:36.940 --> 00:01:38.890
was particularly challenging.

00:01:38.890 --> 00:01:39.740
- Because it's live action.

00:01:39.740 --> 00:01:40.990
There's no hiding behind

00:01:42.160 --> 00:01:44.410
the original actor's
performance on camera.

00:01:44.410 --> 00:01:45.700
Every little twitch of the eye,

00:01:45.700 --> 00:01:48.550
every little stammer, every
little tick that they have,

00:01:48.550 --> 00:01:50.170
you have to try and pay attention to that

00:01:50.170 --> 00:01:53.230
and make it believable and
sell the English language.

00:01:53.230 --> 00:01:54.616
- [Director] Okay, can you play that back?

00:01:54.616 --> 00:01:55.880
- [Gi-hun] Crazy incompetent.

00:01:55.880 --> 00:01:57.380
- [Narrator] People in
the film industry say

00:01:57.380 --> 00:01:58.740
compared with the past,

00:01:58.740 --> 00:02:01.630
many viewers today expect
higher quality dubbing

00:02:01.630 --> 00:02:03.920
or they'll find it hard to
engage with the content.

00:02:03.920 --> 00:02:08.220
- Your goal is to try and
match the lip movements

00:02:08.220 --> 00:02:09.490
as closely as possible

00:02:09.490 --> 00:02:12.920
while still conveying the
emotions of the character.

00:02:12.920 --> 00:02:14.590
- [Greg] A man just died.

00:02:14.590 --> 00:02:16.720
Come on, this isn't right.

00:02:16.720 --> 00:02:18.220
- [Narrator] Chun, who's Korean American,

00:02:18.220 --> 00:02:20.290
gave us a demo as he
worked with the director

00:02:20.290 --> 00:02:21.740
and an audio engineer.

00:02:21.740 --> 00:02:23.540
He said it took him about 45 hours

00:02:23.540 --> 00:02:25.380
to dub his lines for the nine episodes

00:02:25.380 --> 00:02:27.450
in what was a very detailed process.

00:02:27.450 --> 00:02:29.440
- We go on just for the
"C'mon this isn't right."

00:02:29.440 --> 00:02:31.780
It's the big open vowel sound at the end

00:02:31.780 --> 00:02:32.810
- Yeah.
- 'cause of the Korean,

00:02:32.810 --> 00:02:35.796
so we have to almost lose
the enunciation on right

00:02:35.796 --> 00:02:37.370
- Yeah. Yeah, yeah, yeah.
- to go with it.

00:02:37.370 --> 00:02:39.160
Yeah, yeah, yeah.

00:02:39.160 --> 00:02:40.500
- [Gi-hun] A man just died.

00:02:40.500 --> 00:02:41.900
- Come on, this isn't right!

00:02:42.833 --> 00:02:43.666
- [Director] Yes. Yes.

00:02:43.666 --> 00:02:44.840
- There we go, jeez, thank you.

00:02:44.840 --> 00:02:46.000
- [Director] Beautiful,
beautiful, beautiful.

00:02:46.000 --> 00:02:47.300
(Greg groans)

00:02:47.300 --> 00:02:49.630
- [Catherine] Dubbing's
not a pure translation.

00:02:49.630 --> 00:02:51.040
- [Narrator] Catherine Retat oversees

00:02:51.040 --> 00:02:54.482
Netflix's international
dubbing operations from Paris.

00:02:54.482 --> 00:02:56.890
(Gi-hun's friend speaks Korean)

00:02:56.890 --> 00:02:59.210
- [Catherine] We have to
find the right way of saying

00:02:59.210 --> 00:03:01.047
so it sound very native.

00:03:01.047 --> 00:03:03.940
(machine beeps)
(Gi-hun's friend speaks Thai)

00:03:03.940 --> 00:03:06.010
We have to match those nuances

00:03:06.010 --> 00:03:09.440
and then adapt them to the
language we are dubbing into.

00:03:09.440 --> 00:03:12.384
- Any good son knows that.
- Hey, don't say that!

00:03:12.384 --> 00:03:13.217
I do know.

00:03:14.910 --> 00:03:16.290
- Before the incident.

00:03:16.290 --> 00:03:18.720
- [Narrator] For Netflix, it's
become a business priority.

00:03:18.720 --> 00:03:20.240
Beyond "Squid Game",

00:03:20.240 --> 00:03:22.200
dubbing also fueled the global success

00:03:22.200 --> 00:03:23.740
of foreign language content,

00:03:23.740 --> 00:03:25.230
like the French show "Lupin"

00:03:25.230 --> 00:03:27.447
and the Spanish series "Money Heist".

00:03:27.447 --> 00:03:28.917
- This is our last dance, fatty.

00:03:28.917 --> 00:03:30.130
Make it a good one.

00:03:30.130 --> 00:03:31.700
- Netflix with dubbing,

00:03:31.700 --> 00:03:33.220
it's really in parallel with

00:03:33.220 --> 00:03:36.010
how Netflix has been expanding globally.

00:03:36.010 --> 00:03:37.440
The team has been ramping up

00:03:37.440 --> 00:03:40.593
as the expansion was going on the dubbing.

00:03:41.540 --> 00:03:44.040
- [Announcer] Welcome to IQ Robotics.

00:03:44.040 --> 00:03:46.590
- [Narrator] One of Netflix's
early forays into dubbing

00:03:46.590 --> 00:03:48.140
was with children's content,

00:03:48.140 --> 00:03:50.940
since young kids can't read subtitles.

00:03:50.940 --> 00:03:52.870
- If you've ever tried to make this.

00:03:52.870 --> 00:03:54.340
- [Narrator] Then the
company stepped up efforts

00:03:54.340 --> 00:03:55.900
to offer other dubbed versions

00:03:55.900 --> 00:03:58.670
of English language reality TV shows.

00:03:58.670 --> 00:04:00.360
- But one of the thing that really

00:04:00.360 --> 00:04:04.500
I think Netflix has been
really super innovative

00:04:04.500 --> 00:04:07.550
is that now we also offer English dubbing

00:04:07.550 --> 00:04:09.730
and it's extremely successful.

00:04:09.730 --> 00:04:10.660
- [Narrator] Today it works with

00:04:10.660 --> 00:04:13.870
more than 170 dubbing
studios around the world

00:04:13.870 --> 00:04:16.670
to dub content in 34 languages.

00:04:16.670 --> 00:04:18.450
- Someone do something.

00:04:18.450 --> 00:04:20.690
- [Narrator] But as dubbed
content becomes more ubiquitous,

00:04:20.690 --> 00:04:22.950
so does the risk of criticism.

00:04:22.950 --> 00:04:24.670
Social media users have complained

00:04:24.670 --> 00:04:26.530
that the English version of "Squid Game"

00:04:26.530 --> 00:04:28.270
didn't sound realistic.

00:04:28.270 --> 00:04:31.037
- Negative attitudes
usually come from viewers

00:04:31.037 --> 00:04:32.957
who are not used to dubbing,

00:04:32.957 --> 00:04:34.696
especially English viewers.

00:04:34.696 --> 00:04:37.260
- [Narrator] Sofía Mompeán
teaches translation in Spain

00:04:37.260 --> 00:04:39.220
and for the past decade
has been researching

00:04:39.220 --> 00:04:41.360
how to improve dubbed content.

00:04:41.360 --> 00:04:42.870
She says English speaking viewers

00:04:42.870 --> 00:04:44.260
are more likely to complain

00:04:44.260 --> 00:04:46.990
since they can be easily
distracted by the lip sync.

00:04:46.990 --> 00:04:48.360
- Hey, do you hear that?

00:04:48.360 --> 00:04:50.190
- [Narrator] And that can
make them less engaged

00:04:50.190 --> 00:04:51.330
with the show.

00:04:51.330 --> 00:04:54.030
- There will be sometimes losses as well

00:04:54.030 --> 00:04:57.110
because of course there are
some cultural references

00:04:57.110 --> 00:05:00.823
that cannot be explained
in the dubbed version.

00:05:01.880 --> 00:05:03.270
- [Narrator] Chun said he understands why

00:05:03.270 --> 00:05:05.570
some viewers have been
critical about the show,

00:05:05.570 --> 00:05:08.130
but said dubbing is never
going to be perfect.

00:05:08.130 --> 00:05:10.060
- There's a suspension of disbelief

00:05:10.060 --> 00:05:11.970
that you have to adopt
at the very beginning

00:05:11.970 --> 00:05:14.130
that okay, these voices that I'm hearing,

00:05:14.130 --> 00:05:15.600
they're not matching what I'm seeing,

00:05:15.600 --> 00:05:18.490
I somehow have to bridge
that and be okay with it.

00:05:18.490 --> 00:05:19.550
- [Narrator] Netflix acknowledges

00:05:19.550 --> 00:05:20.750
there's room for improvement

00:05:20.750 --> 00:05:23.050
since dubbing is an evolving art form.

00:05:23.050 --> 00:05:24.510
- Dubbing is like cooking.

00:05:24.510 --> 00:05:26.690
It's technique and it's a craft

00:05:26.690 --> 00:05:30.890
and every country has a bit
of its own way to do things.

00:05:30.890 --> 00:05:34.060
We constantly try to
work with our partners

00:05:34.060 --> 00:05:37.000
to stay true to the original version.

00:05:37.000 --> 00:05:38.100
- [Narrator] And Netflix isn't alone

00:05:38.100 --> 00:05:40.100
in providing more dubbed content.

00:05:40.100 --> 00:05:41.350
Other streaming platforms,

00:05:41.350 --> 00:05:44.380
from Amazon Prime to HBO Max and Disney+,

00:05:44.380 --> 00:05:46.060
have also joined the race

00:05:46.060 --> 00:05:48.200
and the global film
dubbing market is expected

00:05:48.200 --> 00:05:52.050
to hit $3.6 billion by 2027.

00:05:52.050 --> 00:05:54.380
- You will learn how to react
in the case of an unhappy--

00:05:54.380 --> 00:05:55.770
- [Narrator] To improve quality and speed,

00:05:55.770 --> 00:05:57.660
some startups are even developing ways

00:05:57.660 --> 00:05:59.508
to use artificial intelligence.

00:05:59.508 --> 00:06:01.910
(AI Woman speaks Portuguese)

00:06:01.910 --> 00:06:03.990
- That's right. That's how
I got caught in all this.

00:06:03.990 --> 00:06:06.440
- [Narrator] Though Retat
says using real voice actors

00:06:06.440 --> 00:06:09.800
will offer an experience
closer to the original content.

00:06:09.800 --> 00:06:11.930
- [Catherine] I really
believe that our strategy is

00:06:11.930 --> 00:06:15.320
to work locally with the
talent and the voices

00:06:15.320 --> 00:06:17.720
to bring authenticity to the dubbing.

00:06:17.720 --> 00:06:19.670
- [Narrator] In a market
that's hungry for content,

00:06:19.670 --> 00:06:21.670
entertainment industry
watchers see dubbing

00:06:21.670 --> 00:06:23.970
as one of the best ways
to bring more shows

00:06:23.970 --> 00:06:26.320
to many more viewers around the world.

00:06:26.320 --> 00:06:27.980
- I look at my job as being

00:06:27.980 --> 00:06:29.760
trying to do the best job possible

00:06:29.760 --> 00:06:32.220
to try and represent the story

00:06:32.220 --> 00:06:33.770
and the performance of the original actor

00:06:33.770 --> 00:06:35.990
as authentically and
as accurately as I can

00:06:35.990 --> 00:06:39.050
and as believably as I can
for the target language.

00:06:39.050 --> 00:06:40.680
Can someone do something?

00:06:40.680 --> 00:06:42.710
So that somebody who
otherwise might not be able

00:06:42.710 --> 00:06:45.530
to enjoy this amazing piece
of storytelling can do so.

00:06:45.530 --> 00:06:47.090
Hey, do you hear that?

00:06:47.090 --> 00:06:50.007
(soft tense music)

